<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The exercise above: if <m>n</m> is an integer that is not divisible by 3, then <m>n^2 = 3k + 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.</p>
<p>Write the two cases with a new letter, <m>n = 3m + 1</m> or <m>n = 3m + 2</m>, where <m>m</m> is an integer. Find <m>k</m> in terms of <m>m</m> in each case.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>If <m>n = 3m + 1</m>, then <m>n^2 = 3k + 1</m> with</p>
<div><m>k =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="ans1"/>  </div></li>
<li><p>If <m>n = 3m + 2</m>, then <m>n^2 = 3k + 1</m> with</p>
<div><m>k =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="ans2"/>  </div></li>
</ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>To show that <m>n^2 = 3k + 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>, exhibit <m>k</m>: expand <m>n^2</m> and write it as 3 times an integer, plus 1.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>Case <m>n = 3m + 1</m>: <m>n^2 = 9m^2 + 6m + 1 = 3(3m^2 + 2m) + 1</m>, so <m>k = 3m^2 + 2m</m>, an integer.</p></li>
<li><p>Case <m>n = 3m + 2</m>: <m>n^2 = 9m^2 + 12m + 4 = 3(3m^2 + 4m + 1) + 1</m>, so <m>k = 3m^2 + 4m + 1</m>, an integer.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>A new letter for the case matters: <m>k</m> is a different integer, built from <m>m</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>3 m^2+2 m</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3 m^2+4 m+1</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>